Task 2 — Internet and Networking (144 words)

When a user in the USA types epicreads.com into their browser, 
the request travels across the internet to the server in Finland.
First, the browser finds the server's IP address, 
the unique numeric address that identifies the machine on the internet,
much like a postal address. The data is not sent as one piece. 
Through packet switching, it is split into small packets, and each packet 
may take a different route through routers and undersea cables before being
reassembled at the destination. TCP/IP makes this reliable: 
IP handles addressing and routing each packet, while TCP ensures 
all packets arrive in the correct order and resends any that are lost. On top of this, the browser and 
server communicate using HTTP, the language for requesting and delivering web pages.
HTTPS adds encryption, so the user's login details and payment information stay private while crossing countries.

